			},
			"date": "2023-09-11T20:54:52.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@philhawksworth\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>philhawksworth</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@jaredwhite\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jaredwhite</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.xyz/@remotesynth\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>remotesynth</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@brittneypostma\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>brittneypostma</span></a></span> Thanks for the context!</p><p>If I wasn't on socials much I would just happily keep on SSG'ing my sites with client-side fetching/rendering when necessary.</p><p>Do we need the Jamstack community to be saved for me to keep doing that?  Do architecture patterns need communities and marketing behind them for developers to use them?</p><p>(Those are semi-non-rhetorical questions you could ask the panel of smart folks if you feel inclined 😀)</p>",
